      "text": "To determine the maximum possible number of planes that can be uniquely determined by 12 points in space, we need to consider how points can be placed to maximize the number of unique planes formed. Specifically, we need to maximize the number of combinations of three points that are non-collinear.\n\n1. **Choosing 3 points out of 12**: The number of ways to choose 3 points from 12 is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{12}{3} \\). This is calculated as follows:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12!}{3!(12-3)!} = \\frac{12!}{3! \\cdot 9!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 220\n   \\]\n   So, there are 220 ways to choose 3 points from 12.\n\n2. **Interpreting the result**: Each set of 3 points uniquely determines a plane. Since any set of 3 points in space, when chosen from 12, will form a unique plane if and only if they are non-collinear, the number of planes determined by these 12 points is exactly the number of ways to choose 3 points out of 12.\n\nTherefore, the maximum possible number of planes that can be determined by 12 points in space is \\(\\boxed{220}\\).",
