      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the maximum possible number of planes that can be formed by 12 points in space, we need to calculate the number of combinations of 3 points chosen from 12. This is a classic \"combination\" problem, where the order of selection does not matter.\n\nThe number of ways to choose 3 points out of 12 is given by the combination formula:\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12!}{3!(12-3)!} = \\frac{12!}{3! \\cdot 9!}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this by canceling out the \\(9!\\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10 \\times 9!}{3! \\times 9!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = \\frac{1320}{6} = 220\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the maximum possible number of planes that can be determined by 12 points in space is \\(\\boxed{220}\\).",
